---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Technical documentation — Solipsism Browser"
  description="Installation, Android permissions, browser-core behavior, privacy boundaries, development commands, and troubleshooting for Solipsism Browser."
  path="/docs/"
>
  <Header />
  <main id="main">
    <section class="archive-hero shell reference-hero">
      <p class="kicker">Technical reference · v9.4.0</p>
      <h1>Understand the<br /><em>browser underneath.</em></h1>
      <p>
        Solipsism is an Android application built around Android WebView. This
        guide covers installation, runtime requirements, permissions, the
        optional Antares core, development, and known limits.
      </p>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button" href={`${base}guides/getting-started/`}
          >Start with the beginner guide</a
        >
        <a
          class="button button-secondary"
          href="https://github.com/Kenneth-Cho-InfoSec/Solipsism">View source</a
        >
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="shell reference-layout">
      <article class="reference-content">
        <section id="requirements">
          <p class="kicker">01 · Install</p>
          <h2>Requirements and releases</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Android 8.0 (API 26) or newer.</li>
            <li>
              An installed Android System WebView provider for the stable
              browser core.
            </li>
            <li>
              The official 9.4.0 release contains one APK asset. Later releases
              may change their assets or minimum requirements; check the release
              notes before installing.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Get APKs from the project’s
            <a href="https://github.com/Kenneth-Cho-InfoSec/Solipsism/releases"
              >GitHub Releases</a
            >
            page. Only install packages from a distribution channel you trust. Android
            asks you to approve installs from the selected source; keep that permission
            limited to the source you use.
          </p>
          <p>
            The package ID is <code>com.krystelligence.solipsism</code>. The
            repository describes Solipsism as MPL 2.0; see the
            <a
              href="https://github.com/Kenneth-Cho-InfoSec/Solipsism/blob/main/LICENSE"
              >license file</a
            >.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="browser-cores">
          <p class="kicker">02 · Rendering</p>
          <h2>WebView and Antares</h2>
          <p>
            Android WebView is the stable core. Web rendering, Safe Browsing,
            and some capabilities depend on the installed WebView provider and
            Android version.
          </p>
          <p>
            Solipsism 9.4.0 also includes Antares 0.5.0 as an experimental,
            opt-in browser core. An official signed ARM64 companion package may
            be used as a fallback where supported. Antares is not required to
            run Solipsism, and WebView remains available as the stable choice.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Antares requires Android 13 or newer and a supported 64-bit ARM or
              x86 device.
            </li>
            <li>
              The companion engine must have the expected package identity,
              signing certificate, and protocol version before Solipsism enables
              it.
            </li>
            <li>
              Switching cores applies to all tabs in the session. History
              stacks, form state, media state, and cookies are engine-private
              and are not promised to carry over.
            </li>
            <li>
              Some complex sites can have incomplete interaction, CAPTCHA, or
              media playback in Antares. Choose WebView if a page does not work.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The project’s
            <a
              href="https://github.com/Kenneth-Cho-InfoSec/Solipsism/blob/main/docs/ANTARES_ENGINE.md"
              >Antares engine notes</a
            >
            describe package verification, supported devices, switching, and the optional
            coordinate diagnostic. That diagnostic loads the same HTTP or HTTPS page
            in a hidden WebView and makes additional requests; it is disabled by default.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="permissions">
          <p class="kicker">03 · Android access</p>
          <h2>Permissions and prompts</h2>
          <p>
            Solipsism declares a small set of permissions and requests several
            others only when a feature needs them. Android or the WebView
            provider can show additional prompts.
          </p>
          <div
            class="reference-table-wrap"
            role="region"
            aria-label="Permission purposes"
            tabindex="0"
          >
            <table>
              <thead
                ><tr
                  ><th>Permission</th><th>Purpose described by the project</th
                  ></tr
                ></thead
              >
              <tbody>
                <tr
                  ><th scope="row">Internet</th><td
                    >Load webpages and access online resources.</td
                  ></tr
                >
                <tr
                  ><th scope="row">Network state</th><td
                    >Check connectivity state.</td
                  ></tr
                >
                <tr
                  ><th scope="row">Notifications</th><td
                    >Show download and scan notifications.</td
                  ></tr
                >
                <tr
                  ><th scope="row">Camera</th><td
                    >QR scanning and optional WebRTC video capture.</td
                  ></tr
                >
                <tr
                  ><th scope="row">Microphone and audio settings</th><td
                    >Optional WebRTC audio capture and audio controls.</td
                  ></tr
                >
                <tr
                  ><th scope="row">Location</th><td
                    >Website location requests when enabled and allowed by
                    Android.</td
                  ></tr
                >
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Site-permission controls marked Experimental are best-effort policy
            signals where WebView does not expose independent enforcement. Do
            not treat them as a fully enforced desktop browser sandbox.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="privacy-and-downloads">
          <p class="kicker">04 · Data and downloads</p>
          <h2>Privacy controls have limits</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Incognito browsing and separate cookie handling are available.
            </li>
            <li>
              Cookies, history, cache, and web storage can be cleared
              individually or on exit.
            </li>
            <li>
              JavaScript, popups, cookies, and selected request headers have app
              controls.
            </li>
            <li>
              Safe Browsing is enabled when the installed WebView provider
              supports it.
            </li>
            <li>
              Navigation to sensitive Android schemes and app-generated local
              pages has additional checks.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Downloads use Android DownloadManager and browser handling. The
            malware scanner combines local definitions with optional VirusTotal
            assistance. Detection can be incomplete; the app itself warns that
            this is an aid, not a replacement for other protections. Only use
            the remote scan option if you understand the data sent to that
            service and have configured your own key.
          </p>
          <p>
            Userscripts are executable code. The manager supports common
            metadata and lifecycle hooks, not the full Tampermonkey API. Only
            install scripts whose source you trust.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="developer-commands">
          <p class="kicker">05 · Development</p>
          <h2>Build and test commands</h2>
          <p>
            The repository README lists these Android development tasks. They
            require the Android SDK, JDK 17 or newer, and the project’s compile
            SDK; they are not needed to install the released APK.
          </p>
          <pre><code>./gradlew :app:assembleSolipsismBrowserDebug
./gradlew :app:assembleSolipsismBrowserRelease
./gradlew :app:testSolipsismBrowserDebugUnitTest
python3 scripts/check_localized_resources.py</code></pre>
          <p>
            Release signing credentials are kept outside the repository. Do not
            add signing keys, API keys, or private configuration to commits. The
            source README and CI workflow contain the current build details.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="troubleshooting">
          <p class="kicker">06 · Troubleshooting</p>
          <h2>Common issues</h2>
          <dl class="troubleshooting-list">
            <dt>A site is blank or missing a feature</dt>
            <dd>
              Check the Android System WebView provider and Android updates. If
              you selected Antares, switch back to WebView and reload.
            </dd>
            <dt>A site permission does not behave as expected</dt>
            <dd>
              Review both Android app permissions and the site-specific setting.
              Some WebView permissions are experimental and may not be
              independently enforceable.
            </dd>
            <dt>An APK will not install</dt>
            <dd>
              Confirm the Android version meets the release requirement, use the
              official release asset, and follow Android’s prompt for that
              installation source.
            </dd>
            <dt>A scan blocks a download</dt>
            <dd>
              Read the detection details and verify the file source. The scanner
              can produce false positives and can miss threats. Do not disable
              scanning for an unknown file just to bypass a warning.
            </dd>
          </dl>
          <p>
            When reporting a problem, include the app version, Android version,
            WebView provider, selected core, and steps to reproduce. Remove
            browsing history, URLs, and personal data from screenshots or logs
            before posting publicly.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>

      <aside class="reference-toc">
        <p class="kicker">On this page</p>
        <nav aria-label="Technical documentation sections">
          <a href="#requirements">Requirements</a>
          <a href="#browser-cores">Browser cores</a>
          <a href="#permissions">Permissions</a>
          <a href="#privacy-and-downloads">Privacy and downloads</a>
          <a href="#developer-commands">Development</a>
          <a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>
        </nav>
        <div class="feature-callout">
          <strong>New to Solipsism?</strong>
          <p>
            Install the official APK and take your first steps with the beginner
            guide.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href={`${base}guides/getting-started/`}
            >Open the guide →</a
          >
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
